::ALG1_U03_UA_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which table shows a linear relationship? {
=A. x: 0, 1, 2, 3; y: 4, 7, 10, 13 #Correct. The y-values increase by a constant amount of 3.
~B. x: 0, 1, 2, 3; y: 1, 2, 4, 8 #The changes are not constant, so this is not linear.
~C. x: 0, 1, 2, 3; y: 5, 5, 7, 10 #The changes are 0, 2, and 3, so they are not constant.
~D. x: 0, 1, 2, 3; y: 9, 7, 6, 6 #The changes are not constant.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.1<br><br>Question:<br>A relationship has a constant rate of change of -2 and starts at 6 when x = 0. Which equation represents it? {
~A. y = 6x - 2 #This reverses the starting value and rate.
~B. y = 2x + 6 #The rate sign is wrong.
=C. y = -2x + 6 #Correct. The slope is -2 and the starting value is 6.
~D. y = -6x + 2 #This uses the wrong rate and starting value.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which description best identifies a linear relationship? {
~A. The output doubles each time #Doubling is not a constant additive change.
=B. The rate of change is constant #Correct. Linear relationships have a constant rate of change.
~C. The graph curves upward #A curved graph is not linear.
~D. The y-values are always positive #Positive outputs do not guarantee a linear relationship.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.1<br><br>Question:<br>A line passes through (0, 3), (1, 5), and (2, 7). What is the rate of change? {
~A. 1 #The x-values change by 1, but the y-values change by 2.
~B. 3 #This is the starting y-value, not the rate.
~C. 5 #This is one y-value, not the constant change.
=D. 2 #Correct. y increases by 2 each time x increases by 1.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which scenario is linear? {
~A. A population doubles every year #Repeated multiplication is not a linear pattern.
=B. A taxi costs $4 to start plus $2 per mile #Correct. A fixed start and constant per-mile rate create a linear model.
~C. A ball's height curves over time #Curved motion is not represented by a linear relationship.
~D. A square's area changes as side length changes #Area depends on side length squared, not a constant rate.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.3<br><br>Question:<br>What is the y-intercept of y = 3x - 8? {
=A. (0, -8) #Correct. The y-intercept occurs when x = 0.
~B. 3 #This is the slope.
~C. (-8, 0) #This reverses the intercept coordinates.
~D. 8 #The sign of the intercept is negative.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.3<br><br>Question:<br>To graph y = -2x + 5, where do you start? {
~A. (-2, 5) #This combines slope and intercept as a point.
~B. (5, 0) #This reverses the y-intercept coordinates.
=C. (0, 5) #Correct. The y-intercept is the starting point for slope-intercept graphing.
~D. (0, -2) #This uses the slope as the y-intercept.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which equation has slope 4 and y-intercept (0, -1)? {
~A. y = -x + 4 #This reverses slope and intercept.
~B. y = 4x + 1 #The intercept sign is wrong.
~C. y = -4x - 1 #The slope sign is wrong.
=D. y = 4x - 1 #Correct. Use y = mx + b with m = 4 and b = -1.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which point lies on y = 2x + 1? {
=A. (3, 7) #Correct. 2(3) + 1 = 7.
~B. (3, 6) #This is one less than the correct output.
~C. (1, 2) #2(1) + 1 = 3, not 2.
~D. (0, 2) #The y-intercept is 1, not 2.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which graphing move matches slope -3? {
~A. Up 3, right 1 #That represents a positive slope of 3.
=B. Down 3, right 1 #Correct. A slope of -3 means decrease 3 for every 1 step right.
~C. Right 3, up 1 #That represents slope 1/3.
~D. Down 1, right 3 #That represents slope -1/3.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FN.5<br><br>Question:<br>What is the slope between (1, 4) and (3, 10)? {
~A. 2 #This is the change in x, not the slope.
~B. 6 #This is the change in y, not divided by the change in x.
~C. 14 #This adds y-values instead of finding rate of change.
=D. 3 #Correct. (10 - 4)/(3 - 1) = 6/2 = 3.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FN.5<br><br>Question:<br>A car travels 180 miles in 3 hours. What is the unit rate? {
~A. 3 miles per hour #This uses the time as the rate.
~B. 54 miles per hour #This does not divide 180 by 3 correctly.
=C. 60 miles per hour #Correct. 180 divided by 3 equals 60.
~D. 177 miles per hour #This subtracts instead of dividing.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FN.5<br><br>Question:<br>A line passes through (2, 9) and (5, 3). What is its slope? {
=A. -2 #Correct. (3 - 9)/(5 - 2) = -6/3 = -2.
~B. 2 #The magnitude is right but the sign is wrong.
~C. -6 #This is the change in y before dividing by change in x.
~D. 3 #This is the change in x, not the slope.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FN.5<br><br>Question:<br>Which situation has a rate of change of 5? {
~A. Starts at 5 and increases by 2 each day #The rate is 2, not 5.
~B. Starts at 5 and stays at 5 #The rate is 0.
~C. Starts at 2 and decreases by 5 each day #The rate is -5, not 5.
=D. Starts at 2 and increases by 5 each day #Correct. Rate of change is the repeated increase.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FN.5<br><br>Question:<br>What does slope represent in a context? {
~A. The starting value only #The starting value is the y-intercept.
=B. The rate of change #Correct. Slope describes how the output changes per 1-unit input change.
~C. The total number of inputs #Slope is not a count of inputs.
~D. The x-intercept #Slope is not where the graph crosses the x-axis.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.2<br><br>Question:<br>Write the equation of a line with slope -4 and y-intercept (0, 6). {
~A. y = 6x - 4 #This reverses slope and intercept.
~B. y = 4x + 6 #The slope sign is wrong.
=C. y = -4x + 6 #Correct. Use y = mx + b with m = -4 and b = 6.
~D. y = -6x + 4 #This uses the wrong slope and intercept.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.2<br><br>Question:<br>A line has slope 2 and passes through (3, 11). What is its equation? {
=A. y = 2x + 5 #Correct. 11 = 2(3) + b, so b = 5.
~B. y = 2x + 11 #This treats the y-value as the y-intercept.
~C. y = 3x + 2 #This reverses the point and slope information.
~D. y = 2x - 5 #The intercept sign is wrong.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.2<br><br>Question:<br>A plan costs $10 plus $5 per month. Which equation models total cost y after x months? {
~A. y = 10x + 5 #This reverses the fixed cost and monthly rate.
=B. y = 5x + 10 #Correct. The rate is 5 per month and the starting cost is 10.
~C. y = 15x #This treats the sum as a rate.
~D. y = x + 15 #This uses a rate of 1.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.2<br><br>Question:<br>A line has slope -1 and passes through (4, 2). What is b in y = -x + b? {
~A. 2 #This treats the y-value as the intercept.
~B. 4 #This treats the x-value as the intercept.
=C. 6 #Correct. 2 = -4 + b, so b = 6.
~D. -2 #This subtracts instead of solving for b.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.2<br><br>Question:<br>A line starts at -3 and increases by 7 per input step. Which equation matches? {
~A. y = -3x + 7 #This reverses rate and starting value.
~B. y = 3x + 7 #This uses the wrong starting value.
~C. y = 7x + 3 #The starting value sign is wrong.
=D. y = 7x - 3 #Correct. The slope is 7 and the y-intercept is -3.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.5<br><br>Question:<br>Which slope is parallel to a line with slope -2? {
~A. 2 #This changes the sign, so it is not parallel.
~B. 1/2 #This is related to a perpendicular slope, not parallel.
~C. -1/2 #This is the negative reciprocal of 2, not the same slope.
=D. -2 #Correct. Parallel lines have the same slope.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.5<br><br>Question:<br>Which slope is perpendicular to 3? {
=A. -1/3 #Correct. Perpendicular slopes are negative reciprocals.
~B. 3 #This is parallel, not perpendicular.
~C. 1/3 #This is the reciprocal but not the negative reciprocal.
~D. -3 #This changes the sign but not the reciprocal.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.5<br><br>Question:<br>Which equation is parallel to y = 4x - 1 and passes through (0, 8)? {
~A. y = -4x + 8 #Parallel lines need the same slope.
~B. y = 1/4x + 8 #This uses the reciprocal slope.
=C. y = 4x + 8 #Correct. Same slope 4 and new y-intercept 8.
~D. y = -1/4x + 8 #This is perpendicular, not parallel.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.5<br><br>Question:<br>Which equation is perpendicular to y = -2x + 5 and has y-intercept (0, 1)? {
~A. y = -2x + 1 #This is parallel to the given line.
=B. y = 1/2x + 1 #Correct. The negative reciprocal of -2 is 1/2.
~C. y = 2x + 1 #This changes the sign but does not use the reciprocal.
~D. y = -1/2x + 1 #This uses the reciprocal but the sign is wrong.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.5<br><br>Question:<br>Which pair of slopes represents perpendicular lines? {
=A. 5 and -1/5 #Correct. The product is -1.
~B. 5 and 5 #Equal slopes are parallel.
~C. 5 and 1/5 #These are reciprocals but not negative reciprocals.
~D. -5 and -5 #Equal slopes are parallel.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q26::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q26<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x + 8 < 15. {
~A. x < 23 #This adds 8 instead of subtracting.
~B. x > 7 #The direction does not change when subtracting.
~C. x > 23 #This uses the wrong operation and direction.
=D. x < 7 #Correct. Subtract 8 from both sides.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q27::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q27<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 4x - 3 >= 17. {
~A. x >= 14 #This stops after subtracting incorrectly.
=B. x >= 5 #Correct. Add 3, then divide by 4.
~C. x <= 5 #The direction does not reverse when dividing by positive 4.
~D. x <= 14 #This uses the wrong operation and direction.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q28::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q28<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve -3x > 12. {
~A. x > -4 #This forgets to reverse the inequality.
~B. x < 4 #The boundary sign is wrong.
=C. x < -4 #Correct. Divide by -3 and reverse the symbol.
~D. x > 4 #This has the wrong sign and direction.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q29::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q29<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which graph represents x <= 6? {
=A. Closed circle at 6, shade left #Correct. The value 6 is included and smaller values are solutions.
~B. Open circle at 6, shade left #Open circle would not include 6.
~C. Closed circle at 6, shade right #This represents x >= 6.
~D. Open circle at 6, shade right #This represents x > 6.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q30::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q30<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve -2 < x + 1 <= 5. {
~A. -1 < x <= 6 #This adds 1 instead of subtracting 1.
=B. -3 < x <= 4 #Correct. Subtract 1 from all three parts.
~C. -3 <= x < 4 #This reverses which endpoint is included.
~D. x < -3 or x >= 4 #This changes an interval into an or statement.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q31::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q31<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which graph description matches y > x - 2? {
~A. Solid line y = x - 2, shade above #The shading is right, but the boundary should be dashed.
~B. Dashed line y = x - 2, shade below #The line type is right, but the shading is wrong.
=C. Dashed line y = x - 2, shade above #Correct. Greater than uses a dashed boundary and shades above.
~D. Solid line y = x - 2, shade below #Both the line type and shading are wrong.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q32::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q32<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.3<br><br>Question:<br>What boundary line is used for y <= -3x + 4? {
~A. y = 3x - 4 #This changes both signs.
~B. y = -3x - 4 #The intercept sign is wrong.
~C. x = -3y + 4 #This switches variables.
=D. y = -3x + 4 #Correct. The boundary comes from replacing the inequality with equality.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q33::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q33<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which symbol uses a dashed boundary line? {
~A. <= #This includes equality, so the boundary is solid.
=B. < #Correct. Strict inequalities do not include the boundary.
~C. >= #This includes equality, so the boundary is solid.
~D. = #An equation is graphed with a solid line.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q34::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q34<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which graph description matches y >= -x + 6? {
=A. Solid line, shade above #Correct. Greater than or equal to includes the line and shades above.
~B. Dashed line, shade above #The shading is right, but equality requires a solid boundary.
~C. Solid line, shade below #The line type is right, but the shading is wrong.
~D. Dashed line, shade below #Both the line type and shading are wrong.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q35::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q35<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which inequality matches a solid line y = 2x + 1 shaded below? {
~A. y < 2x + 1 #This shades below but uses a dashed boundary.
~B. y > 2x + 1 #This uses a dashed boundary and shades above.
~C. y >= 2x + 1 #This uses a solid boundary but shades above.
=D. y <= 2x + 1 #Correct. Less than or equal to uses a solid boundary and shades below.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q36::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q36<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.4<br><br>Question:<br>Does (1, 4) satisfy y <= 3x + 1? {
~A. No, because 4 is greater than 1 #The full right side is 3(1) + 1.
~B. No, because equality is not allowed #Equality is allowed with <=.
=C. Yes, because 4 <= 4 #Correct. Substituting gives a true statement.
~D. Yes, because 1 <= 4 #This compares the wrong values.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q37::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q37<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.4<br><br>Question:<br>A budget is at most $50. x items cost $5 each and y items cost $10 each. Which inequality models the situation? {
=A. 5x + 10y <= 50 #Correct. The total cost cannot exceed 50.
~B. 5x + 10y >= 50 #At most means less than or equal to, not greater than or equal to.
~C. 10x + 5y <= 50 #This reverses the item costs.
~D. x + y <= 50 #This ignores the different costs.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q38::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q38<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.4<br><br>Question:<br>What does a point in the shaded region of 2x + y >= 12 represent? {
~A. A combination that makes 2x + y less than 12 #That would be outside the solution region.
~B. A point that must be on the boundary line #The shaded region includes more than the boundary.
~C. A point where x and y are both 12 #The inequality does not require both variables to equal 12.
=D. A combination that satisfies the inequality #Correct. Points in the shaded region are solutions.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q39::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q39<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.4<br><br>Question:<br>Does (2, 3) satisfy 4x + y < 10? {
~A. Yes, because 2 + 3 < 10 #This ignores the coefficient 4.
=B. No, because 4(2) + 3 = 11 and 11 < 10 is false #Correct. The point is not in the solution region.
~C. Yes, because 4(2) + 3 = 10 #The arithmetic is incorrect; it equals 11.
~D. No, because strict inequalities have no solutions #Strict inequalities can have many solutions.
}

::ALG1_U03_UA_Q40::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_UA_Q40<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.4<br><br>Question:<br>In a graph of y < -2x + 5, what does the dashed boundary tell you? {
~A. Points below the line are not solutions #Below the line is the solution side for this inequality.
~B. The graph has no solution region #There is still a shaded solution region.
=C. Points on the boundary line are not included #Correct. A dashed line means equality is not part of the solution set.
~D. The slope is undefined #The boundary line has slope -2.
}
